<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">

    <!--
        ZenVizor design tokens. The semantic-name keys (e.g. surface.card,
        accent.default, radius.card, text.subtitle) are the contract between
        Claude Design mockups and Claude Code XAML. Mockup annotations literally
        name the token; this file is where that name resolves to a concrete
        value.

        Sources of truth (see CLAUDE.md "Design system: source of truth"):
          - THIS FILE is canonical for the app.
          - docs/design/colors_and_type.css is canonical for Claude Design mocks.
          - The crosswalk in that CSS header documents value differences and
            is the bridge between the two. The two must not drift; when a
            token value changes here, change it there and vice versa.

        v1 brush strategy: every brush token aliases an existing Wpf.Ui Color
        resource via Color="{DynamicResource <WpfUiColor>}". Result: Wpf.Ui's
        own theme dictionary swap (Light/Dark, OS accent change) re-resolves
        the underlying Color and our aliased brush picks up the new value
        automatically.

        WHY DynamicResource at the Color level (not StaticResource): the
        DynamicResource lookup re-fires on theme changes, so our aliased
        brush picks up the new Color the moment Wpf.Ui swaps its theme
        dictionary. StaticResource would snapshot at load and break theming.
        The aliasing approach is honest about a limit: tokens that the brand
        wants to differ from Wpf.Ui's auto-tinted values (e.g. accent.fill =
        constant brand violet in BOTH themes) carry a CONCRETE Color value
        instead of aliasing — they don't theme-swap, which is the intended
        behavior for accent.fill (AA against white in both themes).

        High contrast: handled by a separate ResourceDictionary
        (HighContrast.xaml) that overrides the semantic tokens onto
        SystemColors brushes when SystemParameters.HighContrast is true. App
        startup is responsible for merging it on/off; see HighContrast.xaml
        header for the wiring contract.

        Dotted x:Key literals (accent.default, surface.card, radius.card) are
        preserved exactly. Do NOT PascalCase them or strip the dots — the
        literal string is the contract that lets a mockup annotation map
        mechanically to a {DynamicResource ...} / {StaticResource ...}
        reference.
    -->

    <!--  Surfaces  ============================================================
          Cards/data surfaces over the Mica backdrop need to be opaque enough
          to guarantee text legibility (WCAG AA). surface.card aliases to
          Wpf.Ui's SolidBackgroundFillColorBase, which is an opaque solid (not
          a translucent fill), so text on top is not subject to Mica showing
          through. surface.background is the page background — Mica's job to
          handle, so it aliases the application background color.

          surface.layer is the OPAQUE flyout/section grouping surface. Per the
          Acrylic decision (see docs/design-system.md), the App-Detail flyout
          is OPAQUE surface.layer, not a frosted panel — WPF has no per-element
          backdrop, and a translucent flyout over Mica would make its text
          contrast wallpaper-dependent. Real Acrylic is reserved for OS-level
          surfaces only (tray menu, context menus). -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.background" Color="{DynamicResource ApplicationBackgroundColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.card"       Color="{DynamicResource SolidBackgroundFillColorBase}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.card.alt"   Color="{DynamicResource CardBackgroundFillColorDefault}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.layer"      Color="{DynamicResource LayerFillColorDefault}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.subtle"     Color="{DynamicResource SubtleFillColorSecondary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.subtle.alt" Color="{DynamicResource SubtleFillColorTertiary}" />

    <!--  Text  ================================================================
          Wpf.Ui's TextFillColor* keys are auto-tinted per theme. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.primary"    Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.secondary"  Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorSecondary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.tertiary"   Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorTertiary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.disabled"   Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorDisabled}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.inverse"    Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorInverse}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.on-accent"  Color="{DynamicResource TextOnAccentFillColorPrimary}" />

    <!--  Accent  ==============================================================
          accent.default / .secondary / .tertiary alias Wpf.Ui's SystemAccent*
          for now (matches OS accent). Brand target is to override these to
          violet always — that migration is a separate decision because it
          affects every focus ring and primary button in the app.

          accent.fill is the brand's answer to the dark-accent contrast issue:
          a CONSTANT brand violet (#6d3fd1 = violet-600) used for accent
          SURFACES that carry on-accent (white) text — filled buttons, pills,
          selection indicators. Constant value in BOTH themes; in dark theme
          this is one stop DARKER than accent.default (which can be lighter
          for text/borders/focus), so white text clears AA 4.5:1 on every
          accent.fill surface regardless of theme. DO NOT use accent.default
          as the background of a filled button — on dark theme it's lighter
          (violet-500 in the brand spec) and fails AA against white. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.default"   Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorPrimary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.secondary" Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorSecondary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.tertiary"  Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorTertiary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.fill"      Color="#6D3FD1" />

    <!--  Status  ==============================================================
          Generic status semantics (success/caution/critical/neutral) plus
          ZenVizor-specific connection-state tokens. The service-status dot in
          MainWindow is hardcoded Brushes.DarkOrange / Brushes.MediumSeaGreen
          today; status.connected / .warming / .disconnected are the tokens
          that should replace those during the polish pass.

          status.caution.text is the AA-safe text colour on a caution tint
          background. The plain status.caution is bright amber (for dots /
          graphics / icon fills); using it as text on the soft caution
          background fails AA at small body sizes in light theme. The brand
          value for caution.text in light is #8A5A00 (~5.2:1 on light tint);
          in dark, the bright amber already passes against the dark tint and
          can be reused, but we encode them separately so the migration to
          theme-aware brushes is mechanical. v1 carries the light value as a
          constant; HighContrast.xaml overrides to SystemColors.ControlTextBrush. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.success"            Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorSuccess}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.success.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorSuccessBackground}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.caution"            Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCaution}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.caution.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCautionBackground}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.caution.text"       Color="#8A5A00" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.critical"           Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCritical}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.critical.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCriticalBackground}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.neutral"            Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorNeutral}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.neutral.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorNeutralBackground}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.connected"    Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorSuccess}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.warming"      Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCaution}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.disconnected" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCritical}" />

    <!--  Borders  =============================================================
          Wpf.Ui ships ControlElevationBorderBrush as a LinearGradientBrush
          with NO Color counterpart, so we can't alias it via the Color={...}
          pattern. For card/section strokes we use CardStrokeColorDefault
          (which IS a Color resource). Callers that need the gradient-style
          control border continue to reference Wpf.Ui's
          {DynamicResource ControlElevationBorderBrush} directly. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="border.card"   Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefault}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="border.subtle" Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolid}" />

    <!--  Data-viz palette  ====================================================
          SkiaSharp paints used by LiveCharts2 do NOT inherit DynamicResource;
          chart series colors must be applied in code (ChartBuilder) by reading
          these resources via Application.Current.Resources[<key>] and feeding
          them into SKPaints. That wiring is a follow-up; these tokens are the
          contract that wiring code targets.

          Categorical palette (chart.series.1..8, chart.wan, chart.local) is
          the Okabe-Ito colourblind-safe set: deuteranopia + protanopia
          friendly, AA-legible on both Light and Dark Mica. Fixed hex, does
          NOT theme-swap — same in light and dark.

          chart.upSeries and chart.downSeries are the brand's deliberate
          deviation: violet/teal that theme-swap (per docs/design/colors_and_type.css
          and the design-system spec). v1 carries the light-theme brand values
          as a constant; the theme-swap wiring requires a brand-dict refactor
          and is tracked as a follow-up — see docs/design-system.md "Native
          reconciliation". -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.upSeries"   Color="#6D3FD1" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.downSeries" Color="#20B6C6" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.wan"        Color="#0072B2" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.local"      Color="#009E73" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.1" Color="#0072B2" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.2" Color="#E69F00" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.3" Color="#009E73" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.4" Color="#CC79A7" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.5" Color="#56B4E9" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.6" Color="#D55E00" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.7" Color="#F0E442" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.8" Color="#999999" />

    <!--  Chart chrome  ========================================================
          LiveCharts2 paints NONE of the axis/gridline/label/tooltip/legend
          chrome from the UI theme — set them explicitly on every chart. These
          tokens are read by ChartBuilder and fed into SKPaints / axis Label
          paints / tooltip styles.

          Mappings from the design-system spec:
            chart.axis         -> border.subtle (faint stroke for axis line)
            chart.gridline     -> border.subtle at lower alpha (set in code:
                                  read the Color and apply alpha ~0x0B)
            chart.axis.label   -> text.tertiary (de-emphasized label text)
            chart.tooltip.bg   -> surface.card (OPAQUE — never translucent)
            chart.tooltip.text -> text.primary
            chart.legend.text  -> text.secondary

          All theme-swapping (they alias theme-swapping Wpf.Ui Colors). -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.axis"         Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolid}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.gridline"     Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefault}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.axis.label"   Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorTertiary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.tooltip.bg"   Color="{DynamicResource SolidBackgroundFillColorBase}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.tooltip.text" Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.legend.text"  Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorSecondary}" />

    <!--  Spacing scale  =======================================================
          4-based scale, named by pixel value. App set {4,8,12,16,24,32,48} is
          a clean subset of the brand scale; brand adds {20,40,64}. Use
          space.12 (not space.10) for medium spacing — keeps rhythm. Tokens
          slot directly into Margin/Padding via {StaticResource space.16}. -->

    <sys:Double x:Key="space.4">4</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.8">8</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.12">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.16">16</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.20">20</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.24">24</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.32">32</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.40">40</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.48">48</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.64">64</sys:Double>

    <!--  Corner radius  =======================================================
          BRAND values (reconciled from the placeholder Wpf.Ui ladder). The
          crosswalk in docs/design/colors_and_type.css records the migration
          (radius.sm 4->6, radius.md 6->10, radius.lg 8->14, radius.xs new at
          4 for backwards compat with the old radius.sm value).

          SPLIT into scale + semantic roles. Reason: Wpf.Ui's controls share
          a single CornerRadius resource for both controls and cards; pushing
          radius.md to 10 (cards) would drag button/input radius along if
          anything pointed them at radius.md. The semantic role tokens
          (radius.control / radius.card / radius.overlay) name the INTENT, so
          future scale tuning never affects the wrong surface — and the
          eventual override of Wpf.Ui's ControlCornerRadius can point at
          radius.control without ambiguity.

          Usage:
            - Buttons, inputs, chips, inline banners: radius.control (6)
            - Cards, list rows:                       radius.card (10)
            - Flyouts, dialogs, overlays:             radius.overlay (14)
            - Bare scale values exist for one-off needs but prefer the role
              tokens at call-sites.

          Migration note for Wpf.Ui controls: brand wants controls at 6px
          (currently render at Wpf.Ui's 4px). The override is in App.xaml
          merged dictionaries AFTER Wpf.Ui's theme dict:
              <CornerRadius x:Key="ControlCornerRadius">6</CornerRadius>
          (or point ControlCornerRadius at radius.control via an alias). This
          is deferred until the polish-pass call-site sweep so the visual jump
          is auditable in one diff. -->

    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.xs">4</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.sm">6</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.md">10</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.lg">14</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.xl">20</CornerRadius>
    <!-- radius.pill is a sentinel — XAML has no native "fully rounded" value.
         At call-sites that need a pill, set CornerRadius to half the control
         height directly (e.g. a 24-tall chip uses CornerRadius="12"). 9999 is
         the conventional pill value and renders rounded for any control
         height up to that size. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.pill">9999</CornerRadius>

    <!-- Semantic role tokens. These are the keys to USE at call-sites. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.control">6</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.card">10</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.overlay">14</CornerRadius>

    <!--  Typography scale  ====================================================
          BRAND sizes (reconciled from the smaller Fluent ladder shipping
          today). The crosswalk in colors_and_type.css records each delta.

          Only font.size.display renders in font.brand (Nuqun); everything
          from title.large down renders in font.display (Urbanist). -->

    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.caption">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.body">14</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.body.large">18</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.subtitle">20</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.title">28</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.title.large">40</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.display">68</sys:Double>

    <FontWeight x:Key="font.weight.regular">Normal</FontWeight>
    <FontWeight x:Key="font.weight.semibold">SemiBold</FontWeight>
    <FontWeight x:Key="font.weight.bold">Bold</FontWeight>

    <!--  Type ramp Styles  ====================================================
          Keyed TextBlock Styles with ABSOLUTE LineHeight values computed from
          the brand line-height ratios (display 1.12, title-large 1.20, title
          1.28, subtitle 1.40, body-* 1.50, caption 1.40).

          WHY absolute LineHeight not multipliers: WPF's TextBlock.LineHeight
          takes a Double in DIPs, not a multiplier. Setting it explicitly
          gives consistent vertical rhythm across pages even when the font
          falls back. LineStackingStrategy=BlockLineHeight makes LineHeight
          authoritative — without it, ascenders/descenders that overshoot the
          declared line height push lines apart and the rhythm drifts.

          Apply via Style="{StaticResource text.subtitle}" etc. The styles
          target plain WPF TextBlock; the implicit base TextBlock style below
          still sets Foreground app-wide, and these keyed styles inherit from
          nothing (so they don't have to opt in). -->

    <Style x:Key="text.display" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.brand}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="76" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.title.large" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.title.large}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="48" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.title" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.title}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="36" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.subtitle" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.subtitle}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="28" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.body.large" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body.large}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="27" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.body.strong" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="21" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.body" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="21" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.caption" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.caption}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="17" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <!-- Eyebrow: uppercase, tracked, semibold caption — used for tiny
         section labels above a title. Foreground = accent.text role (alias
         accent.default for now; brand spec wants violet-700/violet-300 by
         theme — deferred with the brand-dict migration). -->
    <Style x:Key="text.eyebrow" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.caption}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="17" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorPrimaryBrush}" />
        <!-- WPF has no text-transform: uppercase the source string at the
             call-site (e.g. Text="STATUS"). WPF also has no letter-spacing
             on TextBlock/TextElement — the brand's tracking-wide (0.04em)
             on eyebrows can't be applied here without per-character Runs.
             Accepted limitation; visually OK because eyebrows are small,
             short, and always uppercase. -->
    </Style>
    <!-- Mono / data: NF Code with tabular numerics. Used for rates, byte
         counts, IPs, ports, PIDs — anywhere column alignment of digits
         matters. font.mono ships single-weight Regular only; FontWeight is
         a no-op. -->
    <Style x:Key="text.mono" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.mono}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="21" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--  Density  =============================================================
          Default Fluent grid spacing is too airy for ZenVizor's data-dense
          surfaces (per-app list, sessions, connections). The compact variant
          is the one to apply on those DataGrids. Apply via:

              <DataGrid Style="{StaticResource style.datagrid.compact}" ... />

          density.row.default / .compact are exposed as raw Doubles for cases
          where a custom row template needs to read the row height directly.

          KNOWN MIGRATION ISSUE: the reconciled type sizes (subtitle 20,
          title 28) push above the 22px compact row height. If a future grid
          column header uses text.subtitle, that row clips. Either keep
          column headers at text.body.strong (14, semibold — fits the 22px
          row) or bump density.row.compact to ~30 for grids that need bigger
          headers. Tracked in docs/design-system.md "Native reconciliation". -->

    <sys:Double x:Key="density.row.default">28</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="density.row.compact">22</sys:Double>

    <Style x:Key="style.datagrid.compact" TargetType="DataGrid">
        <Setter Property="RowHeight" Value="{StaticResource density.row.compact}" />
        <Setter Property="MinRowHeight" Value="{StaticResource density.row.compact}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body}" />
        <Setter Property="CellStyle">
            <Setter.Value>
                <Style TargetType="DataGridCell">
                    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,2" />
                    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
                </Style>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--  Implicit base-TextBlock style  ===========================================
          WPF's plain <TextBlock>.Foreground defaults to literal Brushes.Black
          (NOT a DynamicResource), so any TextBlock without an explicit
          Foreground renders black regardless of theme. Wpf.Ui's <ui:TextBlock>
          themes itself via its own implicit style, but plain WPF TextBlocks
          do not — that's what made the Dashboard column headers ("App",
          "Publisher", ...) render dark-on-dark in dark mode.

          An implicit style targeting WPF's TextBlock fixes this app-wide
          without touching every call-site. Implicit styles only apply to the
          EXACT type, so this does NOT shadow <ui:TextBlock>'s style. Any
          TextBlock that sets Foreground locally also wins via WPF property
          precedence (local value > setter). Keyed text.* styles above use
          BasedOn=null implicitly (no explicit BasedOn) and set Foreground
          themselves, so they win when applied. -->

    <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
